<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#nepal-sambat">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nepal Sambat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/879">879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> Sambat is a lunar calendar era of Nepal that begins in 879 CE. Used for centuries in inscriptions, documents and coins of the Thakuri and Malla periods, it remains the traditional calendar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar people</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, whose new year falls in October or November. Tradition credits a merchant, Sankhadhar Sakhwa, with founding it by paying off the people's debts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nepal-sambat.jpg" alt="Nepal Sambat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uray1130 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajimas_in_new_year_parade2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The era began on 20 October 879, in the reign of Rāghavadeva, and its historical origin remains unknown. A 14th-century chronicle says a new era was dedicated to Pashupati in his reign, which led the historian Petech to link it to a sacred event; Baburam Acharya held that it marked the end of the Licchavi dynasty and the founding of the Thakuri. The orientalist Sylvain Lévi derived it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Shaka era</a>, and Gautama V. Vajracharya argued that it began when the Shaka era reached its year 801.</p><p>Folklore tells that the merchant Sankhadhar Sakhwa obtained sand that turned to gold and used it to clear the valley's debts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> of 1645 and 1743 name the era after him, but his historicity is debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Nepal Sambat was the official calendar of Nepal until the end of the Malla dynasty in 1769, and appeared on coins, stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate inscriptions, royal decrees, chronicles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist manuscripts and legal documents. The name was first used in Nepal Sambat 148 (1028 CE). A letter from the Ming emperor to Shaktisimha of Nepal is dated in it, as are gold coins of Pran Narayan of Koch Bihar, and Newar merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> used it in their documents and inscriptions. An intercalary month is added every three years, and traders traditionally opened new account books on its first day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Shahs replaced it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Shaka era</a>, though a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> was dated Nepal Sambat 895 (1775 CE), and Bikram Sambat superseded the Shaka era in 1903. A campaign to restore it began in the 1920s under Dharmaditya Dharmacharya, an activist based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>, and grew after 1951; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panchayat">Panchayat</a> regime arrested its activists, and in 1987 police broke up a New Year run in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. Sankhadhar Sakhwa was declared a national hero on 18 November 1999, and from 11 November 2023 the government included Nepal Sambat in official documents beside Vikram Sambat. Newars mark its New Year with the Mha Puja.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nepal Sambat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Nepal Sambat</b> era of 879 marks that new beginning.</blockquote><a href="/state/licchavi-kingdom-of-nepal">Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thakuri period is conventionally dated from 879, the start of the <b>Nepal Sambat</b> era, though the founder is uncertain; later chronicles name Raghavadeva and credit Gunakamadeva with founding Kantipur, the city that became Kathmandu.</blockquote><a href="/state/thakuri-dynasty">Thakuri dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
